WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
Stated Clearly presents

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:11.160
What is the RNA world hypothesis? If you
were to go back in time a 120

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:17.730
million years, you'd find yourself
in a dinosaur world. 500 million years

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:23.580
ago was a world of trilobites and other
strange sea creatures. 3.4 billion years

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.230
ago was the world of the first living
cells

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:29.220
and if you were to go back further still

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.420
scientists suspect that chains of a
chemical called RNA, or something similar

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:41.160
to RNA, kick-started this entire
beautiful mess that we call life!

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.309
RNA is thought to have given rise to life for several reasons:

00:00:44.309 --> 00:00:49.170
Chains of RNA are found abundantly in all
living cells today, RNA is a closed

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:54.510
chemical cousin to DNA, and with very
little help from researchers, chains of RNA

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:59.820
can replicate evolve and interact with
their environments. While many details

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.229
have yet to be worked out, the RNA world
hypothesis is the simple idea that

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:08.970
somewhere on early planet, perhaps in a
tide pool or hot spring, the Earth's

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:13.950
chemistry was producing random chains of RNA. Once formed they begin

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:19.740
replicating, evolving, and competing with each other for survival. As these chains evolved and

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.340
diversified, some eventually began
cooperating to produce the genetic code

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.530
a wide array of complex proteins, and
even living cells which, from the

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:32.369
perspective of RNA, can actually be
thought of as houses or survival

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:37.920
machines for RNA to live inside. To
understand how RNA chains can interact

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.270
with their environments, replicate, and
evolve; we first need to understand the

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.060
simple process of base pairing.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.590
chains of RNA are made of nucleotides -
small molecules that come in four

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:56.430
different types labeled A, C, U, and G.
The backbone atoms of a nucleotide, shown

00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:00.210
here is a yellow bar, can form strong
chemical bonds with the backbone atoms

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:04.979
of any other RNA nucleotide. This means
that different chains can have

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:09.569
completely different sequences from left
to right. The parts we call the bases of

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:15.480
nucleotides - the colored section labeled
A, C, U, or G - are attracted to other bases

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.950
sort of like a magnet but they're
selective about who they will stick to.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:25.290
G selectively pairs with C, A selectively
pairs with U. When basis find their

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:30.569
matches and stick together, we call it
"base pairing". Researchers have found that

00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:34.019
with a little bit of assistance,
base pairing allows chains of RNA to

00:02:34.019 --> 00:02:35.849
replicate and evolve.

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:40.530
Here's how it works: When a long chain of
RNA is suspended in cool water with high

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:44.819
concentrations of free nucleotides, the
chain can act as a template for its own

00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:48.750
replication. Nucleotides automatically
base pair with their partners on the

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:53.549
existing chain. If their backbone atoms
form chemical bonds with each other (and, by the way

00:02:53.549 --> 00:02:56.730
this is the part that currently requires assistance from researchers,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:00.450
we're not yet sure how this
would have happened in the wild)

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:05.370
a complimentary RNA strand is born - one
with the exact inverse sequence of the

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:10.350
original! If the water is then heated,
paired basis lose their grip allowing

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:15.209
both chains to act as templates when the
cycle repeats. The great thing about this

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:19.829
process is that every other RNA chain
produced as a copy of the original, but

00:03:19.829 --> 00:03:25.590
sometimes mutations slip in. This means
that as these chains compete for survival and

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:31.290
reproduction, true evolution - descent with
modification acted upon by selection - can

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:37.380
operate on chains of RNA. As amazing as
replication is, base pairing also gives

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:42.030
RNA chains a second special ability. When placed in water cool enough for base pairing

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:47.370
but without enough free nucleotides
for replication, chains will fold up and

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.500
base pair with themselves!

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.730
The end result is a complex shape with
certain sticky basis pointing outward

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.250
because they weren't able to find
partners.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:00.810
These sticky, outward-facing bases can
cause unique chemical reactions by

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:05.310
interacting with other molecules in
their environment. A folded chain of RNA

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:11.610
capable of guiding a specific chemical
reaction is what we call a ribozyme. Some

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:14.280
ribozymes break certain molecules apart

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.959
others joined certain molecules together.
A ribozyme's specific function is

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:24.090
determined by its specific shape, and its
shape is determined by its sequence. If a

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:28.770
mutation changes a ribozyme sequence the, shape can be modified and so can its

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:33.570
function. When ribozymes were first
discovered, scientists wondered how

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:37.740
difficult it would be for random chains
of RNA to evolve legitimate survival

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.840
functions. Imagine, for example, a ribozyme that could build nucleotides out of

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:47.250
molecules it finds in its environment.
Across multiple generations, natural

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:50.880
selection could promote and refine this
ribozyme because the chain would tend to

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.050
have access to more free nucleotides than its rivals, allowing it to replicate more

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.380
often. To explore this idea

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.669
researchers at Simon Fraser University
produced a large group of random RNA

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:06.690
chains and examined them to see if any
happened to be able to make nucleotides.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:12.450
Surprisingly, some actually could, but
they weren't very efficient. Researchers

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:17.010
selected out the successful chains and
then use a lab technique called PCR to

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:22.440
quickly replicate them with slight
random mutations. After just 10 rounds of

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:28.020
PCR followed by selection, highly
efficient nucleotide building ribozymes

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:32.760
evolved. These are molecules with the
life-like ability to actively participate

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:37.800
in their own survival! These ribozymes,
and many others produce through similar

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:42.390
experiments, are beginning to blur the
line between living things and simple

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:48.600
chemistry! So to sum things up, the RNA
world hypothesis is the simple idea that

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.229
the first things to replicate and evolve
on our planet, may have been chains of

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:55.090
RNA or something similar to them.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:59.169
While the basic idea of the RNA world
does seem to give us a promising pathway

00:05:59.169 --> 00:06:04.510
to the origin of life, it's still very
much a work in progress. As mentioned, one

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:08.680
of several unsolved problems is: how did
nature get backbone binding to function

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.729
without the special enzymes or lab
techniques we use today? While many

00:06:12.729 --> 00:06:16.870
researchers continue to focus on RNA,
others are investigating alternative

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:20.860
molecules: chemical systems that might
replicate and evolve without assistance

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:25.900
and could have given rise to RNA.
Continual breakthroughs are being found

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:31.810
in both avenues of research.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.810
I'm Jon Perry, and that's the RNA world hypothesis Stated Clearly.

